---
export const prerender = true;
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About this data — Federal Grant Tracker"
  description="Where Federal Grant Tracker's data comes from, how often it updates, and what it doesn't cover yet."
>

<section class="page">
  <p class="page__eyebrow">Data & methodology</p>
  <h1 class="page__title">About this data</h1>
  <p class="page__intro">Federal Grant Tracker is an independent project. Every figure on this site is pulled from public federal data — nothing here is estimated, surveyed, or self-reported by us.</p>

  <div class="block">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grants.gov</strong> — open funding opportunities, forecasted and posted, across federal agencies.</li>
      <li><strong>USASpending.gov</strong> — awarded grants: recipients, amounts, dates, and geography.</li>
      <li><strong>SAM.gov</strong> — entity and assistance-listing details used to match opportunities to programs.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <h2>How often it updates</h2>
    <p>Opportunities and awards are checked on a recurring schedule against each source's own API. When a source updates its records, it typically takes a few hours to appear here — we are not a real-time feed of federal systems, and some agencies report awards on a lag of their own.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <h2>What "opportunity" and "award" mean here</h2>
    <p>An <strong>opportunity</strong> is a funding announcement — it moves through its own lifecycle (forecasted, open, closed, archived) independently of whether any money has been awarded against it yet. An <strong>award</strong> is a specific, funded grant reported separately, usually after an opportunity closes, sometimes over many months. A single opportunity can have zero, one, or many awards reported against it — we show both, but we don't conflate them into one status.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <h2>Known limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pre-award activity — submissions, resubmissions, and applications under review — is not public data anywhere, including here.</li>
      <li>Full grant proposals and itemized budget justifications are not public; we show summary funding figures only.</li>
      <li>Recipient location may reflect a headquarters address rather than where the funded work actually happens.</li>
      <li>Map figures are relative to each other, not an assessment of need, merit, or outcome.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <h2>Not an official government resource</h2>
    <p>Federal Grant Tracker is an independent, non-governmental project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or operated on behalf of any federal agency. For authoritative source data, see <a href="https://www.grants.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grants.gov</a> and <a href="https://www.usaspending.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USASpending.gov</a> directly.</p>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>

<style>
